te the squared distance at each stationary point and compare the results.\n\nMultiple critical points may correspond to the same squared distance. In such cases, like that shown in the graph below, there would be multiple points of minimum distance.\n\n![](images/de/dea83b2804a816eef505810237940e2d9bb8547ffe77549f833ac04823fa6d30.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199507 #edb/ref 1065 7889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199507 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c98fb0ba-d8a2-5069-b3c5-04961452caa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199507 #edb/ref 1067 "The Candidates Test For Multiple Stationary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use differentiation to find the shortest distance between a point and a curve. \n\nFor example, let's find the smallest distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1$ and the point $(5,1),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1f5b350025a0fd0530531cfc887c148459194afe209e472701054c128f1079.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1.** Use the distance formula to create a function $S(x,y)$ representing the *squared* distance between $(5,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\nS = (x-5)^2 + (y-1)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Calculate the first derivative $S'(x)$ and solve $S'(x) = 0$ to find the stationary point(s).\n\nComputing the derivative $S'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where $S'(x) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-8 & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Use the first or second derivative tests to confirm that the stationary point minimizes the squared distance.\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\nS''(4) = 2.\n$$\n\nSince $S''(4) > 0,$ the point $x=4$ is a minimum of $S(x)$ by the second derivative test.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x = 4.$ \n\nFinally, since $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1065 7893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ef45dd95-3bca-5734-a692-3cebc9f37432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330520 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x{log}_{2}⁡2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4020acf4-04e1-5b60-a2f7-a157b1314764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330987 #edb/ref 1080 "{ln}^{2}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330987 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "731eefef-20d5-574f-9043-1000fa8c1ef7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331379 #edb/ref 1080 "2x{log}_{2}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f4ee1b1-d9b2-51c6-abc3-dc91b813f3ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331700 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331700 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331700 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c509c861-23e4-5b8d-8b8d-1044b852afdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331761 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cc2baf64-fef0-5fb3-8a66-3f57f615f35a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186331700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "497ee4c0-825b-5385-b6b4-603172d90ac7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/22/22505181ad0a10e74a158edabc949444055f1deda57286ff63674c32f2dc79c7.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x$  that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0b5476f-0e78-5366-afe4-a0704f4f9240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429376 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429376 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 4218->654. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429376 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050764 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186525209 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525209 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525209 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "006306fb-d1f5-5084-bc23-6f171183965a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525249 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525249 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525249 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1567255f-89d3-537d-9745-05c128c9a70d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525284 #edb/ref 1080 "{3}^{-x/2}\\opera